From: Charles000@aol.com
Date: Thu, 18 Aug 94 11:51:24 EDT
Subject: [#94-8-205] Information-theoretic death

Much conjecture has been devoted to the concept of cybernetic
enhancements, and to some degree, an object oriented approach to
inherent "skills enhancement" of specific physiological traits or
abilities.  At least in the realm of nanotechnology oriented
enhancements, that is, molecular machine devices sent into the 
human body to perform a "corrective chemistry" procedure on a 
particular cell type for increased performance or response 
characteristics, there is no need for "pre-learned" information
transferral.  

In other words, enhancement of sodium ion conduction rates 
through neural interconnection systems, via alteration of molecular porosity
of synaptical interface sites, would allow a
specific sensory system, or kinetic activity/control feedback
loop to "learn" specific stimulus events much faster than
normal, and therefore would preclude the need for introduction
of external "information sets".

Furthermore, the actual "matching" of such experiencial 
information sets to a "foriegn" electrochemical processing 
system (due to the vast variety of subtle physiological differences
between various humans) would introduce many more problems 
than their possible solution could justify. 

The exception to this rule that I might personally justify is in the
realm of event stimulus "memory maps" that may some day be
directly uploaded into an external electronic storage system, and
redownloaded into another individuals brain (particularly in the
thalamus and related inner sub-cortex regions of the brain), where
only "perceptive" and transitory memory funtions are affected, and
not realtime kinetic or sensory interactions.

In this regard, the greatest challenge is not so much in the 
development of neural "taps" or biocompatable connection systems
for such purposes, but rather, the exact geographic mapping of
of neural activity within the brain, in other words, where exactly
to "tap" which ganglionic cluster sets.

However, some progress is being made in this area of research,
most notably, in the area of magnetoencepholography (MEG), where 
extremely minute fluctuations of magnetic fields affected by the
 electro-chemical activity of the brain can be mapped in 3D, with
suprisingly accurate results.  The U. of Washington's neural 
sciences dept. is considered to be one of the leading research sites
for this type of work, and also, I highly recommend looking into 
some of the research papers of Dr. Erwin Meyher (director of the
membrane biophysics dept. of the Maxwell Planck Institute for
Biophysical Chemistry), and also Dr. Bert Sakmann (director of the 
Maxwell Planck Institute for Medical Research), who shared the
Nobel Prize for Physiology in 1991, for their work in identifying
not only the biomechanics of neural transmitters, but more 
importantly (perhaps), the chemical "messenger" systems that 
many of the different cell types within the human body 
communicate with.

There are many aspects of intra-cellular bio-enhancements 
worthy of consideration, but one also has to examine what aspects
of enhancement actually make reasonable sense before 
speculating on a strategy for a system-wide modality 
modification.

Sincerely
Charles Ostman      



