From: hanson@hss.caltech.edu (Robin Hanson)
Date: Thu, 18 Aug 1994 11:23:54 -0700
Subject: [#94-8-211] Does existence exist? 

On reflection my last post on the topic was opaque, and probably wrong.
I apologize to those who may have been tempted to try and parse it.
Let me try again.

Consider the set of all possible/imaginable/self-consistent
"universes", where a "universe" is a total space-time-whatever state
description (including the physical laws applicable there).  Each of
this kind of "universe" may include many other of other kinds of
"universes", such as including many disconnected space-time regions, or
a quantum superposition of many worlds/histories.  A universe might be
specified by a program and a computer it runs on, for those who like to
think in such terms.

A fundamental question one can ask is:  What things in what universes
subjectively experience what?  

One part of an answer to this question could be to say that not all
possible universes "exist", are "actual", or "physically real".  You
might have two otherwise identical possible universes but only things
in the "actual" universe will experience anything.

But one might still have a sensible answer to this fundamental question
without invoking such a distinction.  What things experience what might
be determined by referring only to more local properties of things in
their universes, and not referring to intrinsicly universe-level
properties.  In this sense "existence" need not exist, as Marvin Minksy
reminds us, though it does seem it could exist.

I think we might make the most progress in deciding which of these
approaches we like best by working on our other parts of an answer to
the fundamental question.  It seems to me that there is much we don't
know about what physical arrangements will have the same or similar
experiences.  

For example, imagine we have a program on some computer that manages to
have essentially the same input/output as an ordinary human brain,
responding to the same space-time pattern of sensory input signals with
the same space-time pattern of effector output signals.  If this
program and computer is very analogous to biological human brains,
we will likely believe this brain experiences the same sort of things
we do.  

But I think many people are rather reluctant to believe in similar
experiences, even when the input/output patterns are preserved, if the
implementation of that pattern is different enough.

For example, imagine "playing back" the result of a previous simulation
run.  Would this result in a new experience with the same content, or
would it be the same identical experience?  Imagine having run
bizillions of previous simulations, and interpolating from lookup table
caches from those runs whenever the current situation is similar
enough.  When would such a program have experiences?  Granted
these tables might be huge, but that's not the issue here.

What if an improbable thermal fluctuation created the same pattern, now
across just space instead of space-time, but without the same causal
structure?  Would that pattern experience?

And what transformations preserve the "same" pattern.  Substituting 1
for 0 and vice versa in I/O bit strings might be considered the same.
But if we allow any tranformation, then any physical phenomena might be
considered the "same" pattern.  

As I recall, the book "The Mind's Eye" had a bunch of these
hypothetical cases to consider.

Perhaps I have misunderstood Marvin at some fundamental level.  If so,
maybe he'll point out where I went wrong.

Robin Hanson

