Subject: [#94-8-212] MEDIA: nanotech, cryogenics

As per Ralph Merkle, yes there are a variety of reasonably credible
arguments for intracellular nanodevice "corrective chemistry"
operations which may well be applicable to a very wide array of
tasks, possibly including repair of cryogenically induced cell
damage.  Most notably, however, cell walls, outer protein 
sheath layers, cytoplasm, etc., suffer significant damage, even 
over long duration, gradual cooling to sub-cryogenic temperatures.  
Even though there have been examples of experiments involving
fish, rodents, cats, and even dogs whose body temperatures were
lowered to 30% of normal temperature ranges (and heartrate and
other metabolistic indicators were substantially lowered as 
well),  and successfully revived after a number of days in
"suspended animation", this is a very far cry from true cryogenic
"suspension", and recovery.  

Although it is conceivable that nanotechnology could solve some of
these problems, this is still entirely theoretcal, and a very long
shot at that
 
As I have posted in an earlier email to extropians, and also in
previous articles, it is considered a possibility
by some theorists that aging itself may be inhibited, or at least 
delayed, by the "repair" or modification of certain molecules
(such as DNA polymerase alpha, which is essential for DNA
repair and maintainence, Coenzyme Q10, DHEA, and other
molecules which are directly affected by, or related to various 
aspects of the aging process).  It is here, in the realm of 
"corrective chemistry", administered by molecular "machinery"
designed to seek out and enter specifc cell types, and perform 
these specific chemical maintainance "tasks", that something
resembling a "fountain of youth" may well come to be (at least
for those who are allowed to have access).

This, however, is a much less daunting task than reconstructing 
entire cell structures which have been completely altered, and in
many cases, ruptured or destroyed, by cryogenic exposure. 

Sincerely
Charles


